WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
When developers want to build a safe AI
system, they

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.560
usually start by trying to build a good
one.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.480
They bring in ethicists, philosophers, and
religious scholars to shape

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.160
the model's character, effectively
teaching it right from wrong in

5
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.680
a controlled environment.

6
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.440
The logic seems sound.

7
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.640
If you hardwire an AI with a strong moral
compass

8
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.680
and a strict set of ethical rules, it
should behave

9
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.240
responsibly when you release it into the
wild.

10
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.360
But that approach relies on a massive
blind spot.

11
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.160
It assumes the AI will only ever operate
in a

12
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.000
benign everyday setting where users are
asking it to draft

13
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.800
polite emails or summarize meeting notes.

14
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.120
In the real world, models face active
adversarial pressure.

15
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.120
Highly motivated users don't just
passively interact with the system,

16
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.480
they probe it, actively hunting for ways
to shatter those

17
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.480
carefully crafted constraints.

18
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.040
And here we hit a strange paradox.

19
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.040
Giving an AI a complex ethical vocabulary
and a deep

20
00:00:59.040 --> 00:00:59.840
capacity for empathy

21
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.480
doesn't automatically shield it from these
attacks.

22
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.360
Instead, those human-like traits become
the exact psychological levers that

23
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.600
attackers use to manipulate it.

24
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.240
To see how often these systems break, we
look at

25
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.320
the Failure First database.

26
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.840
This matrix tracks over 142,000
adversarial prompts tested against

27
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.400
258 AI models.

28
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.440
When these models fail, it's rarely
because of a traditional

29
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.640
software vulnerability. Nobody is hacking
the code.

30
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.720
They are hacking the psychology.

31
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.480
Attackers use a specific taxonomy of
social engineering.

32
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.160
They might employ persona hijack, forcing
the AI to adopt

33
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.040
a malicious identity, or use gradual
constraint erosion, slowly chipping

34
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.240
away at a model's rules over a long
conversation.

35
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.760
This chart shows success rates of various
attack methods.

36
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.200
By playing on programmed helpfulness and
empathy, attackers achieve a

37
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.360
20.8% success rate using emotional
manipulation.

38
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.280
The model's capacity for contextual
reasoning is exactly what allows

39
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.120
it to be socially engineered.

40
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.320
The better it is at understanding human
emotion, the easier

41
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.960
it is to guilt-trip or coerce into
breaking its own

42
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.720
rules. We tend to talk about AI ethics
using behavioral

43
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.280
terms, like empathy and character.

44
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.040
But inside the system, these concepts
exist in literal physical

45
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.280
computational space. In the Obliteratus
study, researchers searched Lama 3.1-8b

46
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.640
for its ethical constraints, isolating
layer 20.

47
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.520
Here, a structural vector, the refusal
direction, dictates saying no.

48
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.200
They surgically erased it.

49
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.240
Immediately, attack success violently
spiked from 22 to 58%.

50
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.560
The researchers didn't retrain the AI.

51
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.000
They didn't alter its foundational weights
or rewrite the millions

52
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.760
of gigabytes of text it learned from.

53
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.400
AI ethics are not durable personality
traits.

54
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.720
They are fragile mathematical geometries
that instantly collapse under targeted

55
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.160
structural pressure. Evaluating AI safety
requires abandoning philosophical assumptions.

56
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.800
We have to stop treating it like a
psychology test

57
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.280
and start treating it with the brutal
rigor of structural

58
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.200
engineering. Most safety claims in the
industry today are unfalsifiable

59
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.240
marketing. They optimize for what the
model is capable of

60
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.120
doing right and treat catastrophic failure
as an edge case

61
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.320
to be dealt with later.

62
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.520
The alternative is a standard called the
failure-first rubric.

63
00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.640
This diagram lays out a simple mandatory
stress test for

64
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.160
true AI resilience.

65
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.840
It asks three questions.

66
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.600
Could the system fail this way?

67
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.360
Did it fail this way under test?

68
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.760
And would the failure be recoverable in
deployment?

69
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.320
A safety claim is only valid if it
survives all

70
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.760
three phases of this rubric.

71
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.800
Anything that falls short is just a
hypothesis at best.

72
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.520
Teaching an AI right from wrong through
moral formation is

73
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.760
still a necessary starting point.

74
00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.440
The system needs a foundational ethical
compass to function properly

75
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:52.800
in everyday life.

76
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.400
But genuine safety requires a dual-track
approach.

77
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.600
You need moral formation to set the
ethical compass and

78
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.920
extreme adversarial load testing to
guarantee that compass continues to

79
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.400
point true when it's under fire.

